
https://heavilysauced.github.io/shooting-timer/
# Range Timer

A self-contained, offline-capable shot timer for practical/competitive shooting practice. No backend, no external libraries, no analytics — everything runs in the browser.

## Features

- **Randomized start delay** (adjustable min/max) before the start beep, so you can't anticipate the go signal
- **Frequency-domain shot detection** — analyzes the live mic input as an FFT spectrum (dB per frequency bin) rather than raw loudness, so you can tune detection around a shot's actual acoustic signature and ignore louder-but-different noise (range commands, wind, footsteps, gear clatter)
- **Interactive spectrum view** in Settings: a live dB-vs-frequency display where you drag a threshold line (dB level) and two band handles (the "tone," i.e. frequency range) directly on the graph — or use the matching sliders. A peak readout flashes when the band crosses your threshold, so you can calibrate by making test noise before ever pressing Start
- **Manual mode** fallback if you'd rather log shots by tapping the buzzer or pressing spacebar (also used automatically if microphone access is denied)
- **Split-time ledger** showing each shot's split and cumulative time
- **Par time** — optional second beep at a target time
- **Auto-stop after silence**, or stop manually
- **String history** saved locally on your device (`localStorage`) — nothing is ever uploaded anywhere
- **Installable as an app** (PWA) and works with **no internet connection** after the first load

## Try it locally

Just open `index.html` in any modern browser (Chrome, Safari, Firefox, Edge). Microphone-based detection requires either `localhost` or HTTPS — opening the file directly (`file://`) will still work, but the browser may block mic access, in which case the app automatically falls back to manual mode.

## Deploy to GitHub Pages

1. Create a new GitHub repository and push these files (`index.html`, `manifest.json`, `sw.js`, `icon.svg`, `README.md`) to the root of the `main` branch.
2. In the repo, go to **Settings → Pages**.
3. Under **Build and deployment**, set **Source** to `Deploy from a branch`, branch `main`, folder `/ (root)`.
4. Save. GitHub will publish the site at `https://<your-username>.github.io/<repo-name>/`.
5. Visit that URL once while online. This lets the service worker cache the app and lets your browser remember the microphone permission.
6. After that first visit, the app will keep working with **no network connection at all** — you can even turn on airplane mode. On mobile, use your browser's "Add to Home Screen" option to install it like a native app.

## Using it at the range

1. Tap **Settings (⚙)** — this also requests microphone access and opens the live spectrum view.
2. Calibrate detection: make a few test noises (claps work reasonably well as a stand-in) and watch the spectrum. Drag the **red dashed line** up/down to set the dB trigger level just above the room's normal noise floor. Drag the two **amber handles** left/right to bracket the frequency range where the loudest, most consistent energy shows up for your gun/ammo/environment — this is the "tone" gate, and narrowing it helps the timer ignore lower-frequency thuds (steel targets, footsteps) or higher-frequency chatter that isn't the shot itself. The peak readout flashes red when your band crosses the threshold, confirming it would trigger.
3. Set your delay range and optional par time, then close Settings.
4. Tap **START**. The screen will say "Get ready…" during the random delay, then you'll hear the beep.
5. Fire your string — shots are detected automatically and logged with split times. If detection is unreliable (heavy wind, echoey indoor bay, suppressed host), turn on **Manual mode** and tap the buzzer (or press spacebar) each time you fire instead.
6. Tap **STOP** (or wait for auto-stop after a few seconds of silence) to end the string.
7. Review splits in the ledger, or check **History (☰)** for past strings.

### Why frequency-domain detection

A simple loudness/peak detector can't tell a gunshot from a car door or a steel plate ringing — they can all be loud. This app runs the mic through an FFT (`AnalyserNode.getFloatFrequencyData`) and only looks for energy, above your dB threshold, inside the frequency band you selected. Muzzle report tends to concentrate energy broadly but often with a strong presence a couple kHz up; picking a band that captures your specific gun/ammo while excluding the noise floor of your range is usually more reliable than a single volume threshold. The app also ignores audio during the ~220ms right after the start beep (so it can't hear itself) and enforces a ~130ms minimum gap between detections (so a single shot's decay isn't counted twice).

## Why it works offline

The entire app is one HTML file with inline CSS/JS — there are no CDN fonts, icon packs, or API calls. `sw.js` (a service worker) caches all four files on first load so subsequent visits, even fully offline, are served from the local cache. Shot history is stored in `localStorage`, which also persists offline.

## Files

- `index.html` — the app
- `manifest.json` — PWA metadata (name, icon, theme color) for "Add to Home Screen"
- `sw.js` — service worker that caches the app for offline use
- `icon.svg` — app icon
- `icon.svg` — app icon
